Overlay patch 0055 (D23, port of Shipwright 0065 / D-084): REAL-TIME TICK
CATCH-UP + the pace probe (GS_PACE).

THE MECHANISM. GameEngine::ProcessGfxCommands runs ONE game tick (SM64: 30 Hz)
and then renders N interpolated frames for it, N = interpolation fps / 30
(120 Hz -> 4). Every one of those frames blocks on the display. Nothing in the
loop looks at the wall clock, so a frame that misses its vsync does not drop a
frame -- it stretches the TICK: four frames at 120 Hz = 33.3 ms = 30 Hz; one
16.7 ms frame among them makes it 41.7 ms = 24 Hz, the whole game (and the
audio, which is produced per tick: push_frame -> Start/EndAudioFrame) 20 %
slow. Shipwright's device logs showed it exactly (sim_tps = fps / 6 there).

THE FIX. Keep a debt: real time elapsed minus ticks x tick period (clamped to
[0, one tick]; a stall over 250 ms -- a load, a background, a menu hitch --
resets it, so the game never fast-forwards through a pause). While the debt is
at least one frame period, drop that many of THIS tick's EARLIEST interpolated
frames (never the last one, which is the tick's real state). Skipping frames
shortens the tick, which pays the debt back on the next measurement, so
nothing double-counts. gSohIos.TickCatchup (default 1) is the A/B switch.
visionOS 3D (gSoh3DMode) is left alone (the compositor paces it).

THE PROBE (no behaviour change): one `GS_PACE` line per 10 s, beside GS_PERF,
splitting each 2D frame into
  wait  -- time inside SetupScreenFramebuffer (nextDrawable / background park)
  busy  -- drawable acquired -> SwapBuffersBegin (ImGui, Fast3D interpret,
           texture loads, commit): the game thread's own CPU work
  sleep -- LUS's SyncFramerateWithTime pacer
plus tick wall p50/p95/max, tps, frames skipped, ticks that skipped, debt,
and thermal + power state (Low Power Mode caps the panel at 60 Hz; charging
heats the phone before thermalState moves). gSohIos.PaceStressMs (default 0,
test instrument only) busy-waits that many ms inside each frame, so the
catch-up can be proven on a simulator.

Generate against a vendor tree with 0001..0054 applied and 0055 not applied.
Hunks keep clear of 0008/0016/0031's hunks so the series stays reversible.


